I needed to remove duplicate values from an array while keeping their first appearance. This small JavaScript helper did it:

function unique(items) {
  return [...new Set(items)];
}

It is useful for repeated tags before displaying them.

Today I wrote another way to deduplicate a list while preserving its original order:

const deduplicate = values => Array.from(new Set(values));

It takes repeated primitive values out of the collection. I have a feeling I solved this exact little problem in an earlier project.

The tag picker was showing duplicates, so I used a Set to keep just the first occurrence of each string.

const uniqueTags = tags => [...new Set(tags)];

This is not a new algorithm, but it is a useful little tool to remember the next time a list repeats itself.

Another list needed repeated values removed without changing the order of the remaining items.

function distinct(values) {
  const seen = new Set();
  return values.filter(value => {
    if (seen.has(value)) return false;
    seen.add(value);
    return true;
  });
}

This explicit version makes the first-occurrence behavior easier to follow.
